  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nOur goal is to minimize the objective function 2*n*max(convolution)/sum(sequence)^2. The key idea is to transform this into a constrained optimization problem by considering the convolution of the sequence with itself. The central concept is that the optimal sequence minimizes the variance of the convolution probabilities, effectively spreading the signal energy more evenly across its autocorrelation. We use a physics-inspired approach by modeling the sequence as forces and displacements that seek equilibrium in both time and frequency domains simultaneously.\n\nWe start by formulating the problem through the lens of matched filtering, where the convolution represents a correlation operation. The objective is equivalent to minimizing the peak force (or energy concentration) in the autocorrelation for a unit impulse system. Our strategy involves constructing a sequence that self-diffuses over multiple stages, both in time and frequency, to avoid local minima.\n\nMathematical specification\nLet our sequence be represented as a ray of energy in a system of matched filtering. We define the normalized energy as a probability mass function with sum=1. The convolution operation then represents the inner product over all shifts, and we aim to minimize the maximum value of these inner products.\n\nTo enforce constraints, we use a dual formulation where the sequence satisfies an inverse time-frequency relation. This is achieved by optimizing in the frequency domain (Fourier space) while ensuring the time-domain representation remains valid. The optimization is guided by a soft-maximum function (log-sum-exp) to target multiple peaks in the convolution spectrum effectively.\n\nImplementation procedure\nWe begin by constructing a sequence if no parent is available (using a standard uniform distribution). Otherwise, we validate and normalize the parent sequence. Then, through iterative optimization stages:\n\n1. Fourier-domain flattening: Adjust the sequence's power spectrum to be more uniform, reducing the variance in the convolution.\n\n2. Time-domain mass equalization: Distribute the normalized energy evenly in the time domain.\n\n3. Soft-max gradient descent: Update the coefficients to minimize the convolution variance by targeting multiple high peaks in the spectrum.\n\n4. Coordinated descent: Perform swaps to maintain non-negativity and sum constraint.\n\n5. Final polishing: Use local optimizations to fine-tune the sequence and achieve the minimal objective.\n\nThe soft-max gradient descent function calculates a dynamic temperature (Tau) for each gradient step, scaling down the gradient force as iterations progress. This avoids overshooting during later stages.\n\nWe control the computation budget by dividing time into stages and adjusting stepping sizes. Each stage has a predefined time budget, and the final sequence is validated against the goal upper bound.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\nWe explore multiple sequence representations:\n\n- Uniform initialization: Good for avoiding local minima.\n- Fourier-based equalization: Helps in reducing non-linear dependencies.\n- Variations in stepping and temperature parameters: Adapted to balance between exploration and exploitation.\n\nThese are chosen based on performance metrics and time remaining. We include a small random restart factor (0.1%) for escape.\n\nBudget and robustness\nTotal time 1500 seconds, allocated for:\n\n- Fourier domain:\n  - Time budget 400 seconds for flattening.\n- Normalized space: 600 seconds for mass distribution.\n- Post-amplification: 500 seconds for soft-max optimization.\n- Coordinated descent: 300 seconds for local swaps.\n\nIf time runs critically low, we revert to the best candidate found already.\n\nValidation and return\nFinal candidate is validated by `evaluate_sequence`: checked for non-negativity, sum at least 0.01, and coefficients within [0,1000]. Objective value is directly compared to 1.5027.",
